20†6.3 Throughput L31:     4. cite21†6.4 Measurement Fidelity L32:     5. cite22†6.5 Ablation and Sensitivity Study L33:     6. cite23†6.6 Impact of GPU-Call Forwarding L34:   8. cite24†7 Discussion and Limitations L35:   9. cite25†8 Related Work L36:   10. cite26†9 Conclusion L37:   11. cite27†References L38: cite28†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L39: \nL40: arXiv:2609.30057v1 [cs.DC] 24 Sep 2026\nL41: # KREX: Concurrent Kernel Benchmarking on Shared GPUs via Region-Granular Exclusivity\nL42: Tianyu Feng^{*} Affiliation: HKUST, Hong Kong SAR, China , Haoxuan Yu^{*} Affiliation: HKUST, Hong Kong SAR, China , Tianyuan Wu Affiliation: HKUST, Hong Kong SAR, China , Lingyun Yang Affiliation: Alibaba Group, China , Daocheng Ying Affiliation: Alibaba Group, China , Yuxiao Wang Affiliation: Alibaba Group, China , Ruibo Fan Affiliation: Alibaba Group, China , Yinghao Yu Affiliation: Alibaba Group, China , Guodong Yang Affiliation: Alibaba Group, China , Liping Zhang Affiliation: Alibaba Group, China and Wei Wang Affiliation: HKUST, Hong Kong SAR, China\nL43: ###### Abstract.\nL44: LLM agents automate GPU kernel optimization by repeatedly composing candidates and measuring their duration on real GPUs. Existing systems preserve measurement fidelity by reserving a GPU for an entire agent session or benchmarking command. However, this results in poor utilization because only a small fraction of command execution requires exclusive GPU access. Sharing GPUs could recover this idle capacity, but introduces contention that compromises measurement fidelity and misdirects the agent’s search.\nL45: We present KREX, a runtime for concurrent kernel agent benchmarking with region-granular exclusivity. KREX lets agents mark critical regions involving timing-sensitive operations within a benchmarking command. The runtime then enforces exclusivity within marked regions and allows concurrent execution outside them, achieving high throughput while preserving measurement fidelity.\nL46: To enforce in-region exclusivity, KREX blocks new competing GPU submissions and drains outstanding work before freezing sibling processes and isolating CPU cores, protecting both GPU execution and the host threads that drive measurements. To maximize off-region concurrency, KREX reuses GPU contexts in persistent context processes to avoid repeated, node-wide serialized context creation. We evaluate KREX on NVIDIA and AMD GPUs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nXtrace: High-Fidelity GPU Intra-Kernel Tracing via Binary-Level Instruction Splicing (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.28769v1)\nciteturn85view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.28769v1\",\"lineno\":40}); Total lines: 541\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract. L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Background & Motivation L18:     1. cite8†2.1 GPU Intra-Kernel Tracing L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Existing Tools Incur Significant Fidelity Loss L20:     3. cite10†2.3 Opportunities and Challenges L21:   4. cite11†3 Xtrace L22:   5. cite12†4 Splicing Probe Instructions L23:     1. cite13†4.1 Modeling L24:     2. cite14†4.2 The Splicing Algorithm L25:     3. cite15†4.3 Allocating the Probe’s Registers L26:     4. cite16†4.4 Resolving the Probe’s Hazards L27:     5. cite17†4.5 Tracing with Spliced Probes L28:   6. cite18†5 Evaluation L29:     1. cite19†5.1 Experimental Setup L30:     2. cite20†5.2 Xtrace-Driven Agentic Kernel Optimization L31:       1. cite21†5.2.1 Case 1: Optimizing FA-3 across Tracing Tools L32:       2. cite22†5.2.2 Case 2: Optimizing FA-4 against cuDNN Trace L33:     3. cite23†5.3 Binary Similarity L34:     4. cite24†5.4 Tracing Overhead L35:       1. cite25†5.4.1 Metrics L36:       2. cite26†5.4.2 End-to-end Duration L37:       3. cite27†5.4.3 Dependency Waits L38:     5. cite28†5.5 Reusing Dead-Period Registers L39:     6. cite29†5.6 Splicing Time L40:   7. cite30†6 Conclusion L41:   8. cite31†References L42:   9. cite32†A Appendix L43:     1. cite33†A.1 Trace Result of Closed-Sourced Vendor Kernels. L44:     2. cite34†A.2 Per-Vendor Splicing Details. L45:       1. cite35†A.2.1 NVIDIA GPUs. L46:       2. cite36†A.2.2 AMD GPUs. L47: cite37†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L48: \nL49: arXiv:2609.28769v1 [cs.AR] 23 Sep 2026\nL50: # Xtrace: High-Fidelity GPU Intra-Kernel Tracing\nL51: via Binary-Level Instruction Splicing\nL52: \nL53: DOI: cite38†XXXXXXX.XXXXXXX†doi.org Conference: Make sure to enter the correct conference title from your rights confirmation email; June 03–05, 2026; Woodstock, NY ISBN: 978-1-4503-XXXX-X/2026/06\nL54: Zhuobin Huang email: zhuobin@u.nus.edu Affiliation: National University of Singapore, Singapore , Kai Zhang email: kai.zhang@u.nus.edu Affiliation: National University of Singapore, Singapore , Weihao Cui email: weihao.cui@u.nus.edu Affiliation: National University of Singapore, Singapore , Hongshi Tan email: hongshi@u.nus.edu Affiliation: National University of Singapore, Singapore , Liang Luo email: liangluo@meta.com Affiliation: Meta, USA , Christopher Dewan email: cmd@meta.com Affiliation: Meta, Japan , Shen Li email: shenli@meta.com Affiliation: Meta, USA and Bingsheng He email: dcsheb@nus.edu.sg Affiliation: National University of Singapore, Singapore\nL55: 2026\nL56: ###### Abstract.\nL57: Modern GPU kernels fuse increasingly more work into a single kernel, and intra-kernel tracing has become the mainstream method to profile them. Tracing inserts probes into the kernel to record its runtime states, and the fidelity of the trace determines the efficiency of performance optimization. Unfortunately, existing tools insert probes before compilation. These tools interfere with the compiler’s optimizations, so they trace a different binary from the one the GPU executes.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - SyFI-ServingStudio/ServingStudio · GitHub (https://github.com/SyFI-ServingStudio/ServingStudio)\nciteturn85view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/SyFI-ServingStudio/ServingStudio\",\"pattern\":\"src\"}); Total lines: 380\nNo matching text found for \"src\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - zhengkid/Dream-RSI: The offical repo for \"Dream-RSI: Recursive Self-Improvement through Evolving Worlds\" · GitHub (https://github.com/zhengkid/Dream-RSI)\nciteturn85view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/zhengkid/Dream-RSI\",\"pattern\":\"prepar\"}); Total lines: 288\nNo matching text found for \"prepar\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAReaL-RSI: Adaptive Scaling and Coordination of Recursive Self-Improvement (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.18083v1)\nciteturn85view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.18083v1\",\"pattern\":\"retriev\"}); Total lines: 264\nNo matching text found for \"retriev\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDream-RSI: Recursive Self-Improvement through Evolving Worlds (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.14858v2)\nciteturn85view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.14858v2\",\"pattern\":\"role\"}); Total lines: 3011\nNo matching text found for \"role\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKernelOPT: Dispatch-Aware Agentic Searchfor GPU Kernel Optimization (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.30059v2)\nciteturn85view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.30059v2\",\"pattern\":\"1.23\"}); Total lines: 1426\nL253: Matched (48 L1 + 43 L2 + 4 L3 = 95): within the 3% noise margin, or no optimization was attempted (deployed model is the compiler baseline). Both contribute ${\\approx}1.00\\times$ and are non-regressing by construction.\nL254: \nL255:   * •\nL256: \nL257: Fallback (16 L1 + 34 L2 + 35 L3 = 85): all candidates rejected or no Triton kernels generated; baseline preserved. Root causes (Panel D): library-dominant (75%), other operation types (11%), no Triton kernels (8%), terminal correctness failures (6%).\nL258: All-problems geomeans (including fallback, where each contributes $1.00\\times$): L1 1.40$\\times$, L2 1.15$\\times$, L3 1.07$\\times$ – this is why the optimized-only metric matters for gauging leverage where the optimizer actually acts.\nL259: A. Summary  |    B. Speedup distribution (70 opt.)\nL260: Level  | Total  | Opt.  | Matched  | Fall.  | Geo.  | Max  | Range  | L1  | L2  | L3  | Tot.\nL261: L1  | 100  | 36  | 48  | 16  | 1.40$\\times$  | 88.63$\\times$  | $\\geq$10$\\times$  | 4  | 1  | 0  | 5\nL262: L2  | 100  | 23  | 43  | 34  | 1.15$\\times$  | 33.11$\\times$  | $[5,10)$  | 6  | 4  | 0  | 10\nL263: L3  | 50  | 11  | 4  | 35  | 1.07$\\times$  | 4.12$\\times$  | $[2,5)$  | 7  | 0  | 2  | 9\nL264:  |  |  |  |  |  |  | $[1.5,2)$  | 2  | 2  | 1  | 5\nL265:  |  |  |  |  |  |  | $[1.1,1.5)$  | 8  | 8  | 4  | 20\nL266: All  | 250  | 70  | 95  | 85  | 1.23$\\times$  | 88.63$\\times$  | $[1.03,1.1)$  | 9  | 8  | 4  | 21\nL267: C. Dominant operation (70 optimized)  |     D. Fallback root causes (85 fallbacks)\nL268: --- | ---\nL269: Operation  | L1  | L2  | L3  | Tot.  | Root cause  | L1  | L2  | L3  | Tot.\nL270: Matmul / Linear  | 15  | 9  | 2  | 26  | GEMM-dominant (cuBLAS)  | 1  | 28  | 6  | 35\nL271: Conv / ConvTranspose  | 8  | 13  | 3  | 24  | Conv-dominant (cuDNN)  | 7  | 6  | 13  | 26\nL272: Reduction / Scan  | 9  | 1  | 2  | 12  | Other operation type  | 7  | 0  | 5  | 12\nL273: Norm / Pool / Other  | 4  | 0  | 4  | 8  | No Triton kernels (LSTM/GRU)  | 0  | 0  | 7  | 7\nL274:  |  |  |  |  | E2E correctness failure  | 1  | 0  | 4  | 5\nL275: Total  | 36  | 23  | 11  | 70  | Total  | 16  | 34  | 35  | 85\nL276: Table 1: Comprehensive KernelBench results vs. torch.compile. Panel A: Level summary; Matched includes both correct kernels within the 3% noise margin and cases where no optimization was attempted (deployed model is the compiler baseline). Panel B: Speedup distribution over the 70 optimized kernels. Panel C: Dominant operation breakdown (by the highest-speedup sub-kernel per problem; name-based classification).\nL277: Panel D: Fallback root causes, classified by dominant Triton kernel name among the 73 performance-regression fallbacks (cuBLAS/cuDNN dominance accounts for 75%), plus 7 no-Triton-kernel and 5 terminal-correctness-failure cases.\nL278: #### Speedup analysis.\nL279: \nL280: The speedup distribution is heavy-tailed (Panel B). Table cite109†8 (Appendix cite50†E ) illustrates how NCU bottleneck metrics (compute, memory, occupancy) drive the planner’s strategy selection across all three levels. The largest gains arise from two mechanisms.\nL281: Algorithmic optimization and fusion. The top five speedups span both mechanisms: L1 012 (diagonal matmul, $O(N^{3})\\to O(N^{2})$ row-scaling, 88.63$\\times$), L2 018 (operator fusion and algebraic simplification of a 5-operation chain, 33.11$\\times$), L1 015 (lower-triangular matmul, skip-zero iteration, 20.26$\\times$), L1 014 (upper-triangular solve, 14.04$\\times$), and L1 085 (batched diagonal, 11.38$\\times$).\nL282: Operator fusion. On L2 064, four fused pointwise operations on a $(256,4096)$ tensor eliminate intermediate memory round-trips (8.30$\\times$). L2 022 achieves 7.18$\\times$ via similar epilogue fusion.\nL283: \nL284: Beyond the 250 KernelBench problems, KernelOPT also improves 4/10 official Triton tutorial kernels (best $1.70\\times$) and 5/10 Helion tutorials (best $1.08\\times$); see Appendix cite66†H .\nL285: ### 6.2 Speedup Baselines: vs. Compile and vs. Eager\nL286: \nL287: To provide context for real-world deployment, we report speedups against both torch.compile and eager PyTorch:\nL288: Level  | vs compile (all)  | vs compile (opt-only)  | vs eager (all)  | vs eager (opt-only)\nL289: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL290: L1 (100)  | 1.40$\\times$  | 2.54$\\times$  | 1.72$\\times$  | 2.64$\\times$\nL291: L2 (100)  | 1.15$\\times$  | 1.84$\\times$  | 1.79$\\times$  | 2.82$\\times$\nL292: L3 (50)  | 1.07$\\times$  | 1.37$\\times$  | 1.56$\\times$  | 1.69$\\times$\nL293: Table 2: Geometric mean speedups vs. torch.compile and eager mode, computed directly from the logged Eager/Compile/KernelOPT do_bench timings for each problem. “All” includes fallback cases: for these, the deployed model is torch.compile (vs. compile $=1.0\\times$ by construction; vs. eager $=$ compile’s own measured speedup over eager for that problem). “Opt-only” reports geomean over the 70 successfully optimized kernels only (36 L1, 23 L2, 11 L3). vs.\nL294: eager (all) excludes 12 problems (5 correctness-failure, 7 no-kernels-generated) with no logged timing at all.\nL295: Compile’s own margin over eager (the ratio of “vs eager” to “vs compile” geomeans) is roughly 1.23–1.56$\\times$ here, meaningfully positive but well below the 2.27$\\times$ literature figure for full macro-architectures (cite72†Ansel et al., 2024 ): KernelBench L1/L2 problems are individual operators or short fusion chains, where dispatch/launch overhead consumes a larger fraction of wall time and Inductor has less surface area to find gains than in full pipelines.\nL296: We expect this margin to grow toward the literature figure for larger real-world models.\nL297: ### 6.3 Fallback Analysis\nL298: Panel D of Table cite83†1 classifies all 85 fallback kernels by root cause (name-based heuristic; full breakdown and examples in Appendix cite55†F ). Library dominance accounts for 64/85 (75%): 35 GEMM-dominant and 26 Conv-dominant cases where cuBLAS or cuDNN already operate near peak throughput, so optimizing a thin Triton epilogue only adds dispatch overhead (caught by Gate 4).\nL299: Performance-gate rejections: 15 L1 cases show a real per-kernel NCU improvement (up to $6.21\\times$ on one example) that Gate 4 still correctly rejects once dispatch overhead is included E2E. Seven L3 models (nn.LSTM/nn.GRU) generate zero Triton kernels.\nL300: Five fallbacks are terminal correctness failures (a $\\rho$-threshold rejection, two crashes, a timeout, and an output-shape mismatch), while a further 166 Gate-3 rejections across 46 problems are transient – recovered within a retry loop without failing the overall model (§cite21†4.4 , Appendix cite67†I ).\nL301: ### 6.4 The Multi-Kernel Composition Gap\nL302: Of the 250 problems, 126 are multi-kernel. Comparing each problem’s per-kernel speedups (from the search’s own “multi-kernel results” summary) against its final E2E verdict, 37 of these 126 (29%) show at least one individually-optimized sub-kernel ($>1.03\\times$) whose gain does not survive re-stitching: the E2E model is matched (21 cases) or regressed (16 cases). Two cases are unambiguous – every listed sub-kernel is individually optimized, yet the whole model is not (e.g.\nL303: a single $30.75\\times$ sub-kernel yields only $0.994\\times$ E2E) – and one L3 model has 4 of 6 sub-kernels individually sped up ($10.02\\times$ down to $1.16\\times$) yet still measures $0.93\\times$ E2E. Full case listing in Appendix cite57†F.2 .\nL922: Table 5: Ablation study, combined across all levels ($n{=}50$; “No inductor-aware” runs on L2 only, $n{=}20$). Removing any single component reduces optimization success by 74–84%. This is the row-sum of Table cite140†7 below.\nL923: Config  | Ablated Component  | Flag\nL924: --- | --- | ---\nL925: C0  | Full system (baseline)  | (default)\nL930: C5  | Single iteration ($T{=}1$)  | -T 1\nL931: C6  | Optimize all sub-kernels  | L2 only\nL932: Table 6: Ablation configurations. C0 is the full system with beam width 4, 5 iterations, NCU profiling, optimization memory, and E2E performance gate.\nL933:  | L1 (20)  | L2 (20)  | L3 (10)  |\nL934: Config  | Opt  | Match  | Fall  | Opt  | Match  | Fall  | Opt  | Match  | Fall  | Geomean\nL935: C0 (full)  | 10  | 6  | 4  | 6  | 7  | 7  | 3  | 1  | 6  | 1.94$\\times$\nL936: C1 (no beam)  | 4  | 3  | 13  | 1  | 0  | 19  | 0  | 0  | 10  | 1.78$\\times$\nL937: C2 (no memory)  | 4  | 3  | 13  | 0  | 0  | 20  | 0  | 0  | 10  | 1.23$\\times$\nL938: C3 (no perf gate)  | 3  | 3  | 14  | 0  | 0  | 20  | 0  | 0  | 10  | 1.14$\\times$\nL939: C4 (no NCU)  | 3  | 4  | 13  | 2  | 0  | 18  | 0  | 0  | 10  | 3.16$\\times$\nL940: C5 ($T{=}1$)  | 3  | 3  | 14  | 1  | 1  | 18  | 0  | 0  | 10  | 2.48$\\times$\nL941: C6 (all kernels)  | –  | –  | –  | 0  | 0  | 20  | –  | –  | –  | –\nL942: Table 7: Ablation results summary. The C0 row shows the full-system results on the same 50-kernel subset. “Opt” = optimized (speedup ${>}1.03\\times$), “Match” = within 3% of baseline, “Fall” = fell back to baseline. C6 runs only on L2. Geomean is computed over optimized kernels only. cite142†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Ablation study results. Each bar group shows the number of optimized, matched, and fallback kernels per configuration across L1, L2, and L3 subsets.\nL943: The full system (C0) achieves 19 optimizations; removing any single component reduces this to ${\\leq}5$. C6 (optimize all sub-kernels, L2 only) produces zero optimizations because the LLM wastes attempts replacing vendor library calls.\nL944: #### Sampled kernels.\nL945: \nL946: The 50 problems are selected via weighted random sampling that preserves the outcome-category distribution within each level:\nL947: \nL948:   * •\nL949: \nL950: L1 (20): 002, 003, 006, 007, 009, 012, 015, 022, 028, 036, 039, 042, 043, 049, 059, 064, 075, 086, 094, 095.\nL951: \nL952:   * •\nL953: \nL954: L2 (20): 001, 003, 009, 010, 018, 020, 029, 032, 039, 042, 049, 050, 053, 055, 056, 068, 077, 081, 093, 098.\nL955: \nL956:   * •\nL957: \nL958: L3 (10): 002, 005, 009, 012, 014, 019, 030, 038, 044, 047.\nL959: #### Observations.\nL960: \nL961:   * •\nL962: Beam search (C1): Collapsing to a single chain ($B{=}1$) reduces optimized kernels from 19 to 5. Both $B{=}1$ and $B{=}4$ draw from the same total per-iteration plan budget $N$ (§cite18†4.3 ); $B{=}1$ spends the full budget deepening one optimization direction, while $B{=}4$ spreads it across up to four independently-seeded chains. The gap indicates that breadth across restart points matters more than depth within a single one for this search, not that $B{=}4$ simply evaluates more candidates.\nL963: \nL964:   * •\nL1079: L2  | 013  | 1.00$\\times$  | MAT  | ConvTranspose  | TO     | L2  | 014  | –  | FALL  | Matmul, Red  | G\nL1080: L2  | 015  | 1.00$\\times$  | MAT  | BN, ConvT  |     | L2  | 016  | 1.06$\\times$  | OPT  | ConvTranspose  | TO\nL1081: L2  | 017  | 0.98$\\times$  | MAT  | Conv, IN  | TO     | L2  | 018  | 33.11$\\times$  | OPT  | Linear, Red  |\nL1082: L2  | 019  | 1.09$\\times$  | OPT  | ConvT, GN  | TO     | L2  | 020  | 1.00$\\times$  | MAT  | ConvTranspose  |\nL1083: L2  | 021  | 0.98$\\times$  | MAT  | Conv, GN  |     | L2  | 022  | 7.18$\\times$  | OPT  | Linear/GEMM  |\nL1084: L2  | 023  | 1.03$\\times$  | OPT  | Conv, GN  | TO     | L2  | 024  | 1.06$\\times$  | OPT  | Conv  |\nL1085: L2  | 025  | 1.23$\\times$  | OPT  | Conv  